------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. PURPOSE
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
A 2D, side-view ENGINEERING TEST of a Cessna 172 that loses its engine and glides.
It is NOT a game: no score, no points, no explosions, no crash or landing logic.

The user's only control after engine failure is the ANGLE OF ATTACK (alpha).
The user watches the aircraft glide (with correct nose-up / nose-down pitch), sees live
engineering data in a corner dashboard, and after the run sees engineering plots,
including exactly what happened at the moment of stall.

It is based on a Flight Dynamics course homework (HW1, Cessna 172) with four parts:
  (a) CL, CD, Cm versus alpha
  (b) maximum lift-to-drag ratio
  (c) equilibrium (steady glide) speed V and flight-path angle gamma versus altitude
  (d) time simulation of V(t), gamma(t), h(t), r(t)
plus ONE approved extension: STALL (Section 6).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. STRICT SCOPE RULES
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
INCLUDED:
  - HW1 aerodynamic model (Section 4.2), point-mass longitudinal equations (Section 4.3),
    equilibrium glide (Section 4.5), time simulation (Section 4.6), stall extension (Sec 6).
NOT INCLUDED (do NOT add):
  - wind, gusts, turbulence
  - flaps, landing gear effects, ground effect
  - landing / crash / explosion logic, scoring, achievements
  - CFD or drawn flow fields around the wing
  - atmosphere models other than the one in Section 4.4
  - thrust after engine failure, propeller aerodynamics
  - 3D graphics
This is a different project from the owner's "paper airplane" assignment. Do not mix them.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. SCENARIO (WHAT THE USER EXPERIENCES)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3.1 Setup screen (before the run)
    Inputs (with defaults):
      - Initial altitude h0 ........ default 1000 m   (range 50 .. 5000 m)
      - Initial angle of attack .... default 6.0 deg  (range -2 .. alpha_max_input)
      - Max simulated time ......... default 300 s    (safety limit)
      - Mode selector:
          "Glide test"  (default, interactive, described below)
          "HW part (d)" (non-interactive reproduction: h0 = 10 m, t = 0..10 s,
                         constant alpha, initial V and gamma from equilibrium, r0 = 0;
                         produces the four HW plots V, gamma, h, r vs time)
      - Run button.
    The initial V and gamma are NOT typed by the user: they are computed automatically as
    the equilibrium glide at h0 and the chosen alpha (Section 4.5), r0 = 0.

3.2 Phase 1 — normal flight (about 3 s of screen time)
    - Aircraft flies steadily at the equilibrium state. Propeller shown spinning
      (blurred disc). Status text: "NORMAL FLIGHT".
    - Implementation note: because the HW model has no thrust model, this phase is
      visual only: the state is held at the equilibrium values (or integrated with the
      same equations — at equilibrium it stays constant). Physics time t starts at 0
      at engine failure.

3.3 Phase 2 — engine failure
    - Banner "ENGINE FAILURE" for ~2 s. Propeller spins down over ~1.5 s until two
      blades are clearly visible and stopped. Thrust = 0 from this moment on (it already
      is zero in the glide equations).

3.4 Phase 3 — glide (user controls alpha only)
    - Keyboard: UP arrow = increase alpha, DOWN arrow = decrease alpha.
      Rate: CONFIG alpha_rate_deg_per_s = 4.0 deg/s while held. Also an on-screen slider.
    - alpha is clamped to [alpha_min_input, alpha_max_input] = [-2, 20] deg (CONFIG).
      (Values above alpha_stall are allowed so the user can deliberately stall.)
    - The aircraft sprite is rotated by the pitch angle theta = alpha + gamma.
    - When stall occurs, a red flashing "STALL" warning appears (Section 6) and the event
      is logged.

3.5 End of run
    - The run ends automatically when altitude h <= 10 m (CONFIG h_end = 10 m), or when
      the max simulated time is reached. No landing, no crash.
    - Then: results screen with final distance, flight time, average L/D, minimum speed,
      stall events (count and times), and buttons "Show plots", "Export", "Run again".

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. PHYSICS (FROM THE HOMEWORK)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4.1 Notation and units (SI everywhere internally; angles in radians internally,
    shown in degrees in UI)
      V      true airspeed [m/s]
      gamma  flight-path angle [rad], positive = climbing, negative in a glide
      h      altitude [m]
      r      horizontal distance travelled [m]
      alpha  angle of attack [deg in the aero formulas below; wing-chord reference]
      theta  pitch angle [rad] = alpha + gamma
      m      mass [kg], g gravity [m/s^2], S wing area [m^2], rho air density [kg/m^3]
      q      dynamic pressure = 0.5 * rho * V^2
      L, D   lift and drag [N],  W = m*g

4.2 Aerodynamic coefficients (HW part a) — alpha in DEGREES
      CL(alpha) = 0.20 + 0.096 * alpha
      CD(CL)    = 0.032 + 0.0524 * CL^2
      Cm(alpha) = -0.01449 * (alpha - 2.25)
    Notes:
      - Cm uses an assumed trim angle of 2.25 deg. Cm is computed, displayed and plotted
        only (point-mass model: Cm does NOT enter the equations of motion).
      - HW validity range: -2 .. 16 deg. The linear CL has no stall; stall is added in
        Section 6 and REPLACES CL above alpha_stall.

4.3 Equations of motion (point mass, longitudinal, thrust T = 0)
      L = q * S * CL
      D = q * S * CD
      dV/dt     = -D/m - g*sin(gamma)
      dgamma/dt = (L - m*g*cos(gamma)) / (m*V)
      dh/dt     = V*sin(gamma)
      dr/dt     = V*cos(gamma)
      theta     = alpha + gamma      (used for drawing and display)
    State vector: x = [V, gamma, h, r].   Input: alpha(t) from the user.

4.4 Constants and data (CONFIG). Defaults below; homework values override them.
      m   = 1156.7 kg        (Cessna 172S max gross weight 2550 lb)       [default]
      S   = 16.165 m^2       (174 ft^2)                                    [default]
      g   = 9.80665 m/s^2
      rho : CONFIG density_model
            "isa_troposphere" (default):
                 rho(h) = 1.225 * (1 - 2.2558e-5 * h)^4.2559
            "constant":
                 rho = 1.225
      >>> OWNER ACTION: replace m, S and the density model with the exact homework values
          if they differ. Show a startup log line listing the values in use.

4.5 Equilibrium (steady) glide (HW part c)
    Steady glide at constant alpha: dV/dt = 0 and dgamma/dt = 0 with T = 0 gives
      tan(gamma_eq) = -CD/CL            ->  gamma_eq = -atan(CD/CL)   (requires CL > 0)
      V_eq = sqrt( 2*W*cos(gamma_eq) / (rho(h)*S*CL) )
    Used for:
      - initial conditions of every run
      - HW part (c) plot: V_eq and gamma_eq vs altitude 0..5000 m (at a CONFIG
        alpha, default alpha at (L/D)max), and optionally a family of curves for
        several alphas.
    If CL <= 0 at the chosen initial alpha, refuse to start and show a message.

4.6 Maximum lift-to-drag ratio (HW part b)
      CL_star  = sqrt(0.032 / 0.0524)                  ~ 0.781
      (L/D)max = 1 / (2*sqrt(0.032 * 0.0524))          ~ 12.2
      alpha_star = (CL_star - 0.20) / 0.096            ~ 6.05 deg
    Compute these from the formulas in code (do not hard-code the results) and show
    them in the plots and docs. Theoretical still-air glide distance from height dh:
      r_theory ~ dh * (L/D)  (small-angle), best case dh * (L/D)max.

4.7 Numerical integration
    - SciPy solve_ivp, method "RK23" (equivalent of MATLAB ode23), rtol = 1e-6,
      atol = 1e-8 (CONFIG). NumPy float64.
    - Interactive mode: alpha changes with user input, so integrate in short fixed
      intervals (CONFIG physics_dt = 0.02 s): each frame, integrate from t to t+dt with
      alpha held constant, then update alpha. Rendering is decoupled from physics.
    - Event: stop when h reaches h_end (10 m). Guard against V <= 0.5 m/s (stop with
      message) to avoid division by zero in dgamma/dt.
    - HW part (d) mode: single solve_ivp call over 0..10 s with constant alpha,
      t_eval with 0.01 s spacing.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. LIVE DASHBOARD (corner panel, updated every frame)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    t [s] | V [m/s and kt] | Vs stall speed [m/s] | h [m] | r [m] |
    alpha [deg] | gamma [deg] | theta [deg] | CL | CD | Cm | L/D |
    sink rate -dh/dt [m/s] | STALL flag (green "OK" / red flashing "STALL")
    Also show small grey text: "Assumption: stall model (see docs)".

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. STALL EXTENSION (approved; NOT part of the homework -> label as ASSUMPTION)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
6.1 Parameters (CONFIG)
      alpha_stall_deg = 14.0     (default; alternative 16.0 = edge of HW range)
      post_stall_slope = 0.07    (per degree)
      post_stall_drag  = "hw_formula"   (default)  |  "assumed_rise" (optional)
      CL_max = CL(alpha_stall) from the HW line  -> 0.20 + 0.096*14 = 1.544 at 14 deg
                                                     (1.736 at 16 deg)

6.2 Lift with stall
      if alpha <= alpha_stall:  CL = 0.20 + 0.096*alpha
      else:                     CL = CL_max - post_stall_slope*(alpha - alpha_stall)
      (CL must stay continuous at alpha_stall.)

6.3 Drag after stall
      "hw_formula": CD = 0.032 + 0.0524*CL^2 (same as HW). Known limitation: drag
                    decreases when CL decreases after stall, which is not physical.
                    Document this.
      "assumed_rise": CD = 0.032 + 0.0524*CL^2 + k_d*(alpha - alpha_stall) for
                    alpha > alpha_stall, k_d CONFIG default 0.02 per degree, clearly
                    marked as an illustrative assumption with no source.

6.4 Stall speed (displayed live)
      Vs(h) = sqrt( 2*W / (rho(h) * S * CL_max) )          (1-g stall speed)
      Optional display: Vs_n = Vs * sqrt(n), n = L / W  (load factor).
      Vs depends on rho, so it changes with altitude (TAS increases with altitude).

6.5 Stall detection and logging
      stall_flag = (alpha > alpha_stall)
      Log each transition (onset and recovery) with time, h, V, alpha, CL.
      Mark onsets on all time plots with a red vertical dashed line and label
      "stall onset t = ... s"; shade stalled intervals light red.

6.6 Why alpha_stall = 14 deg (put this reasoning in docs/theory.md)
    - Cessna 172S POH: stall speed flaps up, power off, max weight 2550 lb:
      48 KIAS = 53 KCAS = 27.27 m/s.
    - Back-computed CL_max = 2W / (rho*S*Vs^2) = 1.541 (sea level, rho = 1.225,
      S = 16.165 m^2). Use calibrated airspeed (KCAS), not indicated (KIAS).
    - The HW CL line reaches 1.54 at alpha ~ 13.96 deg -> alpha_stall = 14 deg.
    - At 16 deg the HW line gives CL = 1.736 -> Vs ~ 25.7 m/s ~ 50 kt, ~3 kt below POH
      (~13% CL over-prediction).
    - Check: POH bank-angle stall speeds follow Vs*sqrt(n): 30 deg -> 57, 45 deg -> 63,
      60 deg -> 75 KCAS.
    - JSBSim c172p (whole aircraft, body-axis alpha): CL_max = 1.47 at 16 deg; after the
      peak 1.43 @ 17.2, 1.38 @ 18.3, 1.30 @ 19.5, 1.15 @ 20.6 deg (source of the 0.07/deg
      slope).
    - NACA 2412 (wing root airfoil) section: XFOIL Re = 1e6 cl_max 1.582 @ 16.5 deg;
      section cl_max is higher than whole-aircraft CL_max (finite wing, tail download).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7. VISUALS
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
7.1 View: 2D side view. Sky gradient, parallax clouds, ground line with distance markers
    every 100 m (labels every 500 m). Camera follows the aircraft horizontally and keeps
    it in the left-center third; vertical camera follows altitude smoothly. A small
    altitude scale on the left edge.
7.2 Aircraft sprite
    - Default: "2004 Cessna 172S" side profile, Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0,
      author Er-Jin Jang:
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2004_Cessna_172S.svg
      Alternative: "1956 Cessna 172", CC BY-SA 4.0:
      https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1956_Cessna_172.svg
    - Convert SVG to PNG with transparent background (e.g. cairosvg), fuselage reference
      line exactly horizontal. Remove/mask the drawn propeller from the image.
    - Rotate with pygame.transform.rotate by theta (degrees) about the approximate CG
      (CONFIG cg_pixel_offset). Positive theta = nose up.
7.3 Propeller: separate procedurally drawn element at the nose, rotating with the body.
      Before failure: translucent blurred disc. After failure: spin-down ~1.5 s, then
      two stopped blades.
7.4 "Air feel" (no CFD)
    - Free-stream streak lines: faint short lines/particles moving past the aircraft in
      the direction opposite to the velocity vector (i.e. parallel to the flight path,
      angle gamma), with screen speed proportional to V.
    - Relative-wind arrow in front of the nose + an arc between the arrow and the
      fuselage reference line labeled "alpha = x.x deg".
7.5 Flight path trail: the current run's path drawn as a line; previous runs of the same
    session drawn faintly for comparison.
7.6 Background assets (optional): Kenney "Background Elements", CC0,
    https://kenney.nl/assets/background-elements  — or simple procedural shapes.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
8. POST-RUN ENGINEERING PLOTS (matplotlib)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    Figure 1 (HW a): CL, CD, Cm vs alpha (-2..20 deg), HW linear CL dashed, stall-model CL
                     solid, alpha_stall marked, operating range of the run highlighted.
    Figure 2 (HW b): L/D vs alpha with (L/D)max and alpha_star marked.
    Figure 3 (HW c): V_eq and gamma_eq vs altitude 0..5000 m.
    Figure 4 (HW d / run): time histories V(t), gamma(t), h(t), r(t) — 4 subplots.
    Figure 5 (run): alpha(t), CL(t), L/D(t), and V(t) with Vs(t) overlaid.
    Stall onset lines and shaded stalled intervals on Figures 4 and 5.
    All axes labeled with units; grid on; titles; consistent style.
    Export: PNG (300 dpi) of every figure + CSV log of the run into
            outputs/run_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS/.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
9. LOG FORMAT (CSV, one row per physics step)
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    t, alpha_deg, CL, CD, Cm, L_over_D, V, Vs, gamma_deg, theta_deg, h, r, rho,
    load_factor, stall_flag
    + a separate events.csv: time, event (engine_failure / stall_onset / stall_recovery /
      end), h, V, alpha_deg.

